refactor(storage): split AgentJobStore into ports and Postgres repositories

Move the 108 business operations of cloud_agent/storage/store.py into 15
Postgres* repositories, each behind a Protocol, with the shared conn-level SQL
extracted into cloud_agent/storage/_sql/. This is the mechanical half of the
one-step persistence refactor: the old store still exists and still serves every
caller, so this commit changes no behaviour.

The move is generated rather than retyped. tools/split_store.py slices each
method out of the original with its ast line/column information, requalifies
only module-level names, and emits the result; tools/equivalence_check.py then
proves the emitted method equals the original after the same requalification,
for all 108 operations. The only normalisation is whitespace inside string
literals, because re-indenting a method into a class body changes a SQL
literal's leading indentation without changing the query.

Structure:

- cloud_agent/domain/        execution values, runtime version policy, id minting
- cloud_agent/storage/_sql/  conn-level helpers and shared projections, none of
                             which acquire a connection of their own
- cloud_agent/ports/         one Protocol per capability, generated from the
                             implementations so signatures cannot drift
- tools/split_store.py       the splitter, and the report it writes to
                             docs/storage/method-attribution.txt
- tools/generate_ports.py    the port generator
- tools/equivalence_check.py the proof described above

Notable decisions, all recorded in docs/storage/method-attribution.txt:

- initialize() is retained as a no-op. The design asked for its removal, but
  tests/unit/test_migration_baseline.py asserts it exists exactly once and that
  its body is a docstring plus a return; deleting it would mean weakening a test
  to land a refactor, which this repository's rules forbid. Its disposition is
  its own commit, where the test can be deleted as the subject rather than as
  the cost.
- _THREAD_SANDBOX_SOURCE lives in _sql/rows.py, not _sql/sandboxes.py as the
  design sketched: the shared get_job projection in _sql/getters.py needs it,
  and placing it with the sandbox writes would make the two modules import each
  other.
- get_job becomes _sql/getters.py:get_job_projection(conn, job_id). The
  conversation read path calls it inside its own transaction and
  finalize_session_fork calls it from a fresh connection after its commit, so a
  function taking the caller's connection serves both without either repository
  depending on the other.

Gates on the new code: ruff format, ruff check and pydocstyle pass; all 45 new
modules import; 108/108 operations verify as unchanged.
